WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.400
So very quickly here,

00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:02.060
I'm going to show you

00:00:02.060 --> 00:00:03.100
one of the voice agent

00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:03.910
I built.

00:00:03.980 --> 00:00:06.000
This one specifically is for

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:06.790
MeetingScheduled.

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:08.740
And it could either be

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:10.370
when a user

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:12.300
fills up a form on

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:14.100
your website and then the

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:16.260
voice agent immediately calls

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:16.620
him.

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:18.420
Or when it's an after

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:20.320
-hour service or we create

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:21.620
some sort of an automation

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:24.440
that the user commits an

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:25.480
action and then receives a

00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:25.990
call.

00:00:25.990 --> 00:00:27.790
So if I go here,

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:29.090
we can just test it.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:30.580
Thanks for calling

00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:31.580
MeetingScheduled.

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:32.660
How can I help you

00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:33.210
today?

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:35.020
Yeah, I was just looking

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:36.120
to understand a bit about

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:37.330
your service here.

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:42.000
Sure, I'm happy to help.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:43.460
MeetingScheduled builds digital

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:44.660
phone receptionists for

00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:46.740
businesses, so your calls can

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:47.890
be answered automatically,

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:49.440
even after hours or when

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:50.400
your team is busy.

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:51.500
What kind of business do

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:52.050
you run?

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:53.900
Good.

00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:57.070
I have an outbound agency.

00:01:00.352 --> 00:01:01.130
Thanks for sharing.

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:02.700
When your customers call in,

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:03.840
what do they usually need

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:04.430
help with?

00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:07.900
Well, usually they look to

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:10.120
increase sales and have more

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:12.110
qualified appointments.

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:15.840
That makes sense for an

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:16.570
outbound agency.

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:17.840
Do you find that you

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:19.510
miss calls during busy times,

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:20.940
or do you already have

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:22.480
someone answering every inbound

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:23.030
call?

00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:24.270
Yeah, I do.

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:26.460
So this agent,

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:28.600
we can modify her and

00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:29.890
we can change her voice.

00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:31.370
It would be different language.

00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:32.520
I have an agent that

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:34.300
speaks German and

00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:36.840
different dialects as well in

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:37.390
German.

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:38.970
If you want to try,

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:41.260
you can just call this

00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:43.820
number and you will be

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:45.140
able to try it out

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:46.150
and speak to her.
